<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
    <!--
        Ambient Accent Visual System — base color tokens (ADR-025, docs/PLANS/phase-5-ambient-accent-ui.md
        §1 D-1). Only <Color> elements live here — no Brush/Style (those live in Brushes.xaml,
        Typography.xaml, Controls.xaml — merged, in that order, right after this dictionary; see the
        merge-order remarks on App.xaml.cs's LoadResourceDictionaries(): StaticResource only resolves
        "backwards" along the merge order). Nothing in the app references these tokens yet (commit C1 —
        dictionaries only, no consumers); C2-C7 wire them up.

        Panel-facing tokens (Surface/Text/Border/Overlay/Accent) target the dark, semi-transparent panel
        chrome that already exists today (ChildWindowModel defaults: BackgroundColor "#1E1E1E", TextColor
        "#DDDDDD"). Color.Surface.* is intentionally fully opaque (alpha FF) — SurfaceOpacity is applied
        at runtime by Theming/SurfaceComposer.cs (C2) via PanelSurfaceBrushConverter, not baked into the
        token, so one neutral base color serves every SurfaceOpacity/PanelSurfaceStyle combination.

        Phase 6.1 (design-system-1x.md §3, ADR-047 D-2): the Settings window's own colours (formerly a
        fixed-light group in this file) moved to DesktopOrganizer.UI.Theming.ThemePalette — Palette T,
        "application chrome", is theme-aware (light/dark/high-contrast) and its C# source of truth also
        feeds the tray menu renderer (phase 6.4), neither of which a WPF-only Color resource could do.
        Nothing in Colors.xaml/Brushes.xaml represents the Settings window any more.

        Colors.HighContrast.xaml overlays the Surface/Text/Border/Overlay/Selection/Sidebar groups below
        (every group except Accent — a per-panel user choice, left as-is) when
        SystemParameters.HighContrast is true (D-12; MAJOR-A widened the overlay past Surface/Text/Border).
    -->

    <!-- Surface: neutral panel background — accent is never mixed in here (DoD "accent is local, not a
         full-panel fill"; enforced in code by SurfaceComposer.ComposeSurface(..., Solid, ...) ignoring
         its accent parameter). -->
    <Color x:Key="Color.Surface.Base">#1E1E1E</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.Surface.Elevated">#262626</Color>

    <!-- Text -->
    <Color x:Key="Color.Text.Primary">#DDDDDD</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.Text.Secondary">#AAAAAA</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.Text.Muted">#8A8A8A</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.Text.Disabled">#668A8A8A</Color>

    <!-- Border -->
    <Color x:Key="Color.Border.Default">#33FFFFFF</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.Border.Subtle">#1AFFFFFF</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.Border.Hover">#45FFFFFF</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.Border.Focus">#3B82F6</Color>

    <!-- Overlay: non-accent hover/press washes. Color.Overlay.Hover originally reused the exact literal
         both PanelHeaderButtonStyle's and PanelItemContainerStyle's IsMouseOver trigger painted directly
         (Templates.xaml) — named here ahead of the commit that migrated those triggers onto it, so the
         migration was a pure rename with zero visual change. Both triggers were later moved onto the
         luminance-adaptive HoverOverlayBrush converter (Theming/HoverOverlayPolicy.cs; MAJOR-3 fix,
         docs/PLANS/workspace-grid-07-visual.md fix-cycle 1 and its color-presets-and-size-step.md review
         round), so this token is no longer painted directly by either — Brush.Overlay.Hover (Brushes.xaml)
         now only feeds that converter's High Contrast passthrough fallback (Controls.xaml/Panels.xaml), and
         HoverOverlayPolicy.LightenWash keeps a byte-identical copy of this same literal for
         PanelItemContainerStyle's icon-tile hover on a dark surface (NEW-1 fix,
         docs/PLANS/release-polish-1.0.md). -->
    <Color x:Key="Color.Overlay.Hover">#1AFFFFFF</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.Overlay.Pressed">#26FFFFFF</Color>

    <!--
        Accent palette (ADR-025 §3.1 / Theming/AccentPalette.cs, C2). Kept in sync by construction: the
        seven hex strings below are exactly the literals AccentPalette.cs's C# constants use (Neutral is
        the migration fallback / non-accented default — see AccentPalette.Default — not one of the six
        selectable Presets).
    -->
    <Color x:Key="Color.Accent.Neutral">#6B7280</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.Accent.Blue">#3B82F6</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.Accent.Violet">#A855F7</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.Accent.Amber">#F59E0B</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.Accent.Green">#22C55E</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.Accent.Cyan">#06B6D4</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.Accent.Coral">#F06464</Color>

    <!--
        Selection: shared "selected/active" chrome (Templates.xaml finalization m-1 — see
        Brushes.xaml's SelectionFillBrush/SelectionStrokeBrush, the consumer-facing key names, which
        stay unchanged). Used by Controls.xaml's PanelHeaderToggleButtonStyle (Sort-button highlight)
        and Templates.xaml's FileNameEditBoxStyle (inline-rename editor chrome) — the same fixed blue
        both used before C5, unrelated to any panel's own AccentColor. Named here (rather than left as
        an inline Color on the SolidColorBrush in Brushes.xaml) purely so Brushes.xaml keeps sourcing
        every brush from a Colors.xaml token like its own top-of-file comment documents; the merge
        order (D-1) is *why* this had to move out of Templates.xaml in C5 at all: once
        PanelHeaderToggleButtonStyle relocates to Controls.xaml (merged before Templates.xaml), a
        StaticResource reference from Controls.xaml back to something still declared in Templates.xaml
        (merged after) would be an unresolvable forward reference.
    -->
    <Color x:Key="Color.Selection.Fill">#4D3D8BFF</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.Selection.Stroke">#B33D8BFF</Color>

    <!--
        Selection.Text (MAJOR-2 fix, review round 3): the paired foreground for Color.Selection.Fill
        above. #DDDDDD deliberately matches Color.Text.Primary exactly — in normal mode the Sort
        button's active background is a low-alpha blue wash (#4D3D8BFF) light text already reads fine
        against, so introducing this token changes zero pixels there. It only diverges from
        Color.Text.Primary in Colors.HighContrast.xaml, where it maps to SystemColors.HighlightTextColor
        instead of WindowTextColor — see that file's own remarks for why the un-paired foreground was a
        readability bug in HC mode specifically.
    -->
    <Color x:Key="Color.Selection.Text">#DDDDDD</Color>

    <!--
        Sidebar (SystemIconsPanelWindow, C5): the desktop-icons sidebar's own translucent background
        strip. Deliberately its own token, not a reuse of Color.Surface.Base (that token is fully
        opaque by design, per this file's own top-of-file comment — SurfaceOpacity is applied at
        runtime by SurfaceComposer, not baked in) or the PanelSurfaceBrushConverter/PanelTheme
        machinery ChildWindow uses: the sidebar is not a themed panel with its own AccentColor (D-7 —
        SystemIconsPanelViewModel has no such concept), and swapping in either would change its
        rendered colour, not just name it. Same RGBA value the inline literal already had.
    -->
    <Color x:Key="Color.Sidebar.Background">#55000000</Color>

    <!--
        ADR-030 "Reference-Style Layered Panel Chrome" (docs/PLANS/reference-panel-chrome.md §2.3) —
        non-semantic surface-highlight gradient endpoints (D-11) and the hit-test-catcher's
        near-invisible-but-opaque fill (D-5). Both are pure chrome, never accent — see
        Colors.HighContrast.xaml for their HC overrides (Highlight.* -> Transparent;
        HitTest.Catcher deliberately left un-overridden, see that file's own remarks).
    -->
    <Color x:Key="Color.Chrome.Highlight.Top">#0FFFFFFF</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.Chrome.Highlight.Bottom">#00FFFFFF</Color>

    <!--
        Alpha=1 (not 0): a fully-transparent (alpha 0) pixel on a layered (AllowsTransparency="True")
        window is routed by the OS straight through to whatever is beneath it, bypassing this window's
        WPF hit-test tree entirely (ADR-025 MAJOR-1's own click-through-lens finding). Alpha=1 is
        visually indistinguishable from fully transparent but keeps the OS routing the click to THIS
        window (plan D-5) — PART_HitCatcher's whole reason to exist.
    -->
    <Color x:Key="Color.HitTest.Catcher">#01000000</Color>
<!--
        Overlay scrollbar (docs/prompts/scrollbar-pack, spec 04 "Design tokens"): the custom vertical
        ScrollBar every panel ListBox's ScrollViewer uses (Controls.xaml's Control.ScrollBar.Overlay,
        applied as an implicit style from ChildWindow.xaml's PanelRoot and SystemIconsPanelWindow.xaml's
        root Border — NOT MainWindow.xaml, whose light Settings window stays system-styled, D-9).
        Neutral graphite / frosted grey, deliberately never accent-tinted and never a glow — the bar
        must dissolve into PanelChrome's dark surface (Color.Surface.Base #1E1E1E), not compete with it.
        Track is fully transparent (alpha 00 is fine here: the Track's RepeatButtons carry their own
        Transparent-brush hit-test fill for WPF hit-testing, and the bar sits over the panel's own composed
        surface pixels, so the OS-level layered-window click-through of alpha-0 pixels — the
        PART_HitCatcher alpha=1 concern below — does not apply). Thumb alphas step Normal < Hover < Pressed so the
        hover/drag feedback is a delicate lightening, not a colour change.
    -->
    <Color x:Key="Color.ScrollBar.Track">#00000000</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.ScrollBar.Thumb">#66788088</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.ScrollBar.ThumbBorder">#407F8A90</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.ScrollBar.ThumbHover">#80919BA0</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Color.ScrollBar.ThumbPressed">#A0A8B0B4</Color>
</ResourceDictionary>
